media: h713-hdmirx: the last German strings in English

0136l took the twenty-one German strings and comments that T2 8.2 named and
then counted what was left: 63 more lines in fifteen functions, the whole
release path, the colour-converter path, the init sequence, the streaming
summary and the entire debugfs status file. That was a patch of a different
size, and it had to wait, because 0136m, 0136n and 0136s change the status
file's wording anyway and nothing should be translated twice. They have
landed, so this is the rest.

Counted again on this tree before the change: 66 lines carry a German string
(0136l's 63 plus two "%u mal" in the event counters and one of the two audio
lines that the earlier count took together), and five identifiers are German
as well - wann, erste, heiss, wechsel, stabil. All of them are here.

Same meaning, no behaviour change. Every message keeps its severity, its
format specifiers in the same order, its "--" continuation and its
references; only the words are English now. Where the German used a compound
the English uses the term this tree already uses elsewhere: "Freigabe:"
becomes "release:", "Farbwandler:" becomes "converter:", "Quellenwechsel"
the source switch, "Wiederholung" the repeat, "Flip-Zeiger" the flip
pointers.

Four keys of the debugfs status file are renamed, because their names were
the German words themselves and a key cannot be half translated:

  slot-quelle:  ->  slot-source:
  zeilen:       ->  pitch:
  farbwandler:  ->  converter:
  bild:         ->  picture:

The column the values start in is unchanged (14 characters), so the file
still reads as a table. h713-tv filters the kernel's status file by key
prefix and carries "farbwandler:" in that list; it is changed to
"converter:" in the same commit, and its README with it. The other three
keys are read by nothing in this tree. `format:`, `incap:`, `capture:`,
`signal:`, `timings:`, `colour:` and `hdr:` - the keys h713-tv forwards -
keep their names; only the text after them changes.

One correction rather than a translation, and it is named here rather than
made silently: the give-up message of the repeat told the reader to run
"hy310-tv ctl resync". That program is called h713-tv and has been for
months, so the English line says h713-tv.

The scan that says this is done. `grep -n "[aouAOU umlauts]"` was never the
test for this file: its German is transcribed (ae, oe, ue), which is why
0136l's report says the umlaut grep had been empty all along. What was used
here is a German word list over every line of the driver, transcriptions
included, with the English homographs (in, an, so, die, man, war, byte)
left out of the list and checked by hand instead. Before: 66 lines. After:
three, all of them the file name doku/nachtlog/E2-freigabe.md in a citation
- a document that exists under that name.

Source: umbau/work/Q3/REPORT.txt section 1.1 and follow-up N1, review
umbau/reviews/Q3.md ("63 remaining German lines ... its own patch after the
status file's wording settled").
Follow-up to 0136l, 0136n and 0136s; all three stay byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

